          "clinician": "Defined genetically from Denisova Cave, Altai (Krause et al. 2010; Reich et al. 2010; high-coverage genome, Meyer et al. 2012) as a sister group of Neanderthals. Morphological identifications rely on ancient proteins and DNA: Xiahe mandible (Chen et al. 2019), the Harbin cranium (2025 palaeoproteomic and dental-calculus mtDNA evidence, Fu et al. 2025a, 2025b; originally named Homo longi by Ji et al. 2021) and the Penghu 1 mandible (2025 palaeoproteomics; Tsutaya et al. 2025). A child’s molar from Tam Ngu Hao 2, Laos, is attributed to Denisovans on morphology alone (Demeter et al. 2022). Denisovan-related ancestry forms a few per cent of the genomes of Papuans and Aboriginal Australians and derives from more than one Denisovan source population (Browning et al. 2018; Jacobs et al. 2019); a Denisovan-like EPAS1 haplotype contributes to high-altitude adaptation in Tibetans (Huerta-Sánchez et al. 2014). Brain size is the single Harbin estimate (~1,420 cm³). rangeKa is approximate: earliest evidence at Denisova Cave ~250–200 ka (sediment DNA and fossils; Zavala et al. 2021; Brown et al. 2022); the youngest Denisovan fossil is a rib fragment from Baishiya Karst Cave dated ~48–32 ka (Xia et al. 2024)."
